 "name": "Fireflies",
 "_note": "The instruction about fireflies is that they must not be a uniform particle field, and every number here exists to break one kind of uniformity. SPATIAL: clusterCount 26 with clusterRadius 1.9 draws every spawn from one of twenty-six centres, and a centre is a pure function of its index (ADR-520 clusterRand, which deliberately does not mix the frame -- the first version used the frame-mixing hash and the clusters strobed), so a swarm stays where it is instead of dissolving back into the box it was drawn from. TEMPORAL: pulseRate 1.6 with pulseSharpness 9 is a brief flash with darkness between, not a glow. SOCIAL: pulseSync is the one number that decides whether this is a crowd or a chorus -- 'fly-chorus' sits at 0.88 and flashes almost together, 'fly-scatter' at 0.05 and twinkles at random, and they are the same system with one number changed. MOTION: pauseRate 0.9 with pauseFraction 0.62 means each fly spends most of its cycle nearly still and darts between, on its own phase -- so at any instant part of the swarm is hanging and part is moving, which is the readable difference between a swarm and a snowfall. ATTRACTION AND AVOIDANCE are the existing attractor with a radius: positive on 'fly-chorus' (they gather at the clearing's centre) and negative on 'fly-scatter' (they keep off the camera). Nothing here is a new subsystem; it is ADR-015's attractor, ADR-025's fields and ADR-520's four knobs.",
